  "narrative": "The PTEN context decomposition largely confirms the conditional PTEN signal but clarifies that the overall PTEN-loss contrast is context-dependent rather than uniformly modified. H67/R55 tests PTEN loss in TP53-intact and KEAP1-intact lines: signed mean difference +0.255 composite units with 95% interval +0.165 to +0.345. Because the claim direction is negative raw, the positive signed estimate means PTEN-loss lines have a lower composite score, i.e. stronger measured dependency. The entire interval lies beyond the 0.15-unit threshold in the claimed direction, so I accept H67 and request independent validation for it because the lower bound is only moderately above threshold. H68/R56 is almost as strong: PTEN loss in TP53-intact and SMARCA4-intact lines gives +0.261 with interval +0.172 to +0.350, also fully beyond threshold, so I accept H68. Together with validated H58 and accepted H51, this supports a robust conditional PTEN association in contexts where the dominant TP53-loss and either KEAP1-loss or SMARCA4-loss predictor is absent. I still do not treat the unadjusted PTEN claim H50 as established because its interval crosses the threshold.\n\nThe interaction analyses do not support the originally registered PTEN amplification claims. H66/R54 tests whether PTEN-loss contrast is more negative in TP53-loss-positive lines than in the complement. The signed estimate is -0.157 with interval -0.459 to +0.145. The upper bound is below +0.15, so the data do not support a claimed negative interaction exceeding threshold and in fact rule out a positive-signed claimed effect beyond threshold. Interpreted on the raw scale, the point estimate is opposite the registered claim, suggesting PTEN loss may be attenuated in TP53-loss-positive lines, consistent with H58 versus H59, but the interval is broad and not decisive. I therefore reject H66 as stated and register a reversed-direction PTEN-by-TP53 interaction claim to formalize this possible attenuation without accepting the original hypothesis. H69/R57 tests PTEN-by-KEAP1-loss interaction: +0.145 with interval -0.064 to +0.354. The point is directionally consistent with stronger PTEN loss in KEAP1-loss lines but below threshold and broad, so H69 remains unresolved and active. H70/R58 tests PTEN-by-SMARCA4-loss interaction: -0.107 with interval -0.321 to +0.108. For the claimed negative direction, the upper bound is well below +0.15, so the claimed amplification is ruled out; the small opposite attenuation is not conclusive. I reject and close H70.\n\nCDKN2A remains borderline. H71/R59, after removing TP53-loss and PTEN-loss contexts, gives +0.119 with interval +0.042 to +0.195. This is directionally consistent with earlier CDKN2A estimates but the point estimate is below 0.15 and the interval straddles the threshold. I keep H71 unresolved and deferred. The pattern across H19, H57, H52, and H71 argues against a decisive CDKN2A effect exceeding the prespecified threshold.\n\nThe broad predictor screen gives no new meaningful main effect. H53/R60 BRAF V600E gives +0.049 with interval -0.071 to +0.169; the point is far below threshold although the upper tail barely crosses it, so it remains unresolved but deferred as a low-priority marginal signal. H54/R61 EGFR amplification is -0.092 with interval -0.197 to +0.014, opposite to the claimed negative raw effect and not beyond threshold; reject and close. H55/R62 ERBB2 amplification is +0.018 with interval -0.094 to +0.131, entirely too small to support the claimed effect; reject and close. H56/R63 SMAD4 loss is -0.036 with interval -0.146 to +0.073, also below threshold; reject and close.\n\nThe Avana-library audits are reassuring for KEAP1 but not for the registered positive modification. H48/R64 tests whether the KEAP1-loss effect is larger in Avana than non-Avana. The signed estimate is -0.166 with interval -0.365 to +0.033; the upper bound is far below the +0.15 threshold, so the claimed positive Avana modification is not supported and is excluded beyond threshold. The negative point could indicate possible attenuation in Avana or larger effects in non-Avana lines, but non-Avana cells are small and the effect is not decisive; because the main KEAP1 effect remains accepted and validated, I do not promote this technical heterogeneity further. H49/R65 tests TP53-by-Avana modification: +0.063 with interval -0.256 to +0.382. This is too broad to support any interaction claim, but it does not rule out meaningful heterogeneity; I keep H49 unresolved and deferred. Overall, these results support the core associations, strengthen conditional PTEN inference, and show that broad genomic predictors and the tested technical Avana interactions do not add scientifically meaningful effects beyond the core predictors and the already notable constructed signature interactions.",
  "research_notes": "Updated conclusions and plan. Core accepted and validated: SMARCA4 loss -> lower composite/stronger measured dependency; KEAP1 loss and TP53 loss -> higher composite/weaker measured dependency. PTEN loss is now accepted in TP53-intact (H58 validated), TP53/KEAP1-intact (H67 newly accepted; validation requested), and TP53/SMARCA4-intact (H68 newly accepted), plus triple-negative TP53/KEAP1/SMARCA4 absent (H51). Overall PTEN remains context-specific: H50 unadjusted unresolved; H59 TP53-loss-positive small; H66 registered PTEN amplification in TP53-loss rejected, but raw interaction point suggests attenuation in TP53-loss lines, so reversed PTEN-by-TP53 interaction proposal registered. H69 PTEN-by-KEAP1 remains unresolved; H70 PTEN-by-SMARCA4 amplification rejected. CDKN2A remains borderline and likely subthreshold: H19/H57/H52/H71 all positive but point estimates or lower bounds below 0.15; H71 deferred. Broad screens: BRAF unresolved deferred because upper tail barely crosses threshold; EGFR, ERBB2, SMAD4 rejected closed. Library audits: KEAP1-by-Avana positive modification rejected closed; TP53-by-Avana inconclusive deferred. Validation budget priority: H67 because discovery lower bound is just above threshold and it supports independence of PTEN effect from KEAP1-loss context. Treat all findings as associations with the composite dependency endpoint, not causal single-gene effects or external generalizability; constructed signatures and thresholded subgroups are especially susceptible to multiplicity and post hoc interpretation.",
  "validate": "H67"
}

