  "narrative": "The newly delivered validation results support the original claims H3, H24, and H67. For H3, TP53-loss versus intact, the independent validation signed mean difference is +0.471 composite dependency-score units with a strict interval of +0.334 to +0.607. This is far above the 0.15-unit threshold in the claimed positive direction, meaning raw TP53-loss lines have higher composite scores, i.e. weaker measured dependency. I therefore accept H3 as a validated dataset association and close it. For H24, SMARCA4-loss versus intact within KEAP1-loss lines, the validation signed estimate is +0.926 under a negative-raw claim direction, with strict lower bound +0.741. Because the claim direction is negative, this signed positive estimate means the raw contrast is strongly lower, i.e. stronger measured dependency. The effect is very large and stable across discovery and validation, so I accept H24 and close it. For H67, PTEN-loss versus intact within TP53-intact and KEAP1-intact lines, the validation signed estimate is +0.305, with strict lower bound +0.166. Under the negative-raw claim direction, this corresponds to raw PTEN-loss lines having a lower composite score, i.e. stronger measured dependency. The validation lower bound exceeds the 0.15 threshold, although modestly, and is consistent with discovery R55. I accept H67 and close that conditional PTEN claim.\n\nTaken together, the strongest dataset-supported main/conditional findings are: SMARCA4 loss is associated with stronger measured dependency; KEAP1 loss is associated with weaker measured dependency; TP53 loss is associated with weaker measured dependency; PTEN loss is associated with stronger measured dependency in TP53-intact contexts and in contexts where dominant KEAP1/SMARCA4 effects are absent. However, PTEN loss should not be accepted as a uniform unadjusted main effect. H50 remains unresolved because its direct interval still crosses below the prespecified threshold, and the TP53-loss-positive PTEN stratum H59 remains underpowered and broad. The reversed PTEN-by-TP53 interaction H75 has a point estimate suggesting possible attenuation of PTEN loss in TP53-loss-positive lines, but its interval is too broad for acceptance; I defer it rather than prioritize it further now.\n\nThe main unresolved high-priority signal remains H65, the TP53-loss interaction with high research_signature_a. Its discovery interaction is large and accepted, but it is based on a constructed standardized assay and an analysis-defined threshold, so independent validation is the most informative next assessment. Since this synthesis response form does not include a validation request field, the next appraise step should request validation for H65. I also registered a follow-up mean-difference test: TP53-loss versus intact within the same research_signature_a>=1 stratum, with an uncertain anticipated raw direction. This will clarify whether the accepted negative interaction reflects partial attenuation, near-nullification, or possible reversal of the usual TP53-loss positive raw effect. If raw TP53 loss remains strongly positive within high signature_a, H65 should be interpreted as attenuation; if it is near zero, as near-nullification; if it becomes negative beyond threshold, a new reversed-direction claim should be considered. Other unresolved items such as H44 lineage-subtype interaction, H69 PTEN-by-KEAP1 interaction, and H50 overall PTEN remain lower priority until the constructed-signature interaction is either validated or deprioritized.",
  "research_notes": "Updated conclusions: Validated/accepted core associations in this dataset are SMARCA4 loss -> stronger measured dependency (lower composite); KEAP1 loss -> weaker dependency (higher composite); TP53 loss -> weaker dependency (higher composite). PTEN loss -> stronger measured dependency is accepted in several TP53-intact conditional strata (H58, H67, H68, H51) but not as a uniform unadjusted main effect; H50 remains unresolved and H59 remains deferred. H24 confirms SMARCA4-loss association within KEAP1-loss lines. H65 is accepted but not validated and is the priority validation target in the next appraise because it is a strong constructed-assay interaction. The registered follow-up test asks whether the raw TP53-loss effect within research_signature_a>=1 remains positive, is attenuated, near null, or reversed. Defer H75, H59, H71, H53, and routine library/omics audits unless the signature interaction or PTEN context questions change priorities. Keep all conclusions as associations with a composite dependency endpoint, not causal single-gene effects or external generalizability."
}
